# Limitations

This standard is intentionally narrow about what it can prove.

Its strength comes from making trust boundaries visible, not from pretending those boundaries disappear.

## What it can standardize

It can standardize and expose declared:
- protocol metadata
- disclosure scope
- components
- authority nodes
- privileged powers
- control edges
- delays, thresholds, and bounded parameters
- structural findings inside the declared model
- audit records
- audit applicability
- audit findings
- audit-evidence summaries

It can also standardize helper views that make the disclosure easier for downstream tooling to consume.

## What it cannot prove by itself

It cannot prove:
- that the disclosure is complete if the publisher lies by omission
- that every relevant dependency was included in scope
- that undisclosed controllers do not exist offchain, socially, legally, or operationally
- that a component marked exact-verified truly matches the live system without independent verification
- that a timelock, multisig, or controller is configured exactly as declared unless independently checked
- that a live deployment still matches an audited target unless drift is independently evaluated
- that an audited protocol is safe
- that summaries are equivalent to proof
- that address bindings are sufficient to describe every authority structure

## Why this matters

A dishonest protocol can still publish a neat graph, a neat manifest, and a neat audit section.

So the standard is strongest when paired with:
- exact verification systems such as Sourcify or equivalent independent proof
- live proxy and admin-path discovery
- independent graph reconstruction
- independent verifier reports
- tooling that treats declared facts and independently verified facts as different classes

## Summary-specific limitation

Summary fields are helper views.

They are not:
- canonical raw truth
- a trust badge
- a proof of safety
- stronger than the underlying disclosures

Users and downstream tooling must not confuse:
- a clean summary
with
- independently verified reality

## Audit-specific limitation

Audit evidence is not a trust foundation by itself.

A protocol can have:
- a real audit that covered only part of the system
- a real audit that no longer matches the live deployment
- a real audit with unresolved findings
- a real audit with disputed findings
- a real audit that never meaningfully reviewed the privilege graph
- a real audit that omitted modules, guards, fallback handlers, or control paths that matter now

That is why this repository keeps audit evidence separate from structural findings.

## Identity-model limitation

The canonical model is `nodeId`-based, not purely address-based.

That is necessary because not every authority structure is cleanly representable by a single address.

An address may be:
- absent even when a real authority exists
- present but incomplete as a description of control
- only one projection of a broader authority structure

## Versioning and hash limitation

This repository now treats many hash fields as commitments.

But until a final proposal fixes canonical serialization rules, hash equality alone should not be over-interpreted across independent implementations.

Likewise, `standardVersion` should not be treated as universal semantic compatibility without a published versioning policy.
